--- title
# Velis

--- body
A collaborative workspace designed around documents that remain editable when the network is unavailable.

--- actions
[Product website](https://velis.vu/) · [All products](/en/#products)
:::

::: section topic-1
## Local state

In an offline-capable editor, the first durable copy of a change cannot depend on a successful request to the server. Velis maintains local workspace state and retains pending changes for later exchange with the shared service.

This changes the application model: loading a document, modifying it and synchronizing it are distinct operations. The user can continue editing during a temporary disconnection without treating the network as the only place where work exists.
:::

::: section topic-2
## Synchronization

Reconnecting creates two separate problems: repeated delivery and concurrent edits. The synchronization path therefore has to recognize changes already handled and make conflicts explicit. Collaboration on structured content also requires rules for the order in which operations become visible.

Rather than moving entire documents after each keystroke, the editor works with smaller editing operations. That increases flexibility, but the behavior under simultaneous structural edits is harder to establish than single-user editing.
:::

::: section topic-3
## Engineering boundary

The application separates local persistence, editing behavior, synchronization and workspace permissions. Real-time notifications complement synchronization; they are not a substitute for the durable exchange of changes.

Tests exist for selected offline synchronization, conflict and concurrent-operation scenarios. The remaining question for any local-first product is convergence under sustained, adversarial editing across devices, not whether a connected editor can save a page.
:::

